  "client_view_text": " A write-ahead log is a file that contains a record of all changes made to the database. When a change is made to the database, the change is first written to the log, and then the change is written to the database itself. This ensures that the log always contains a record of all changes made to the database, even if the database itself is not updated.\nWhen a crash occurs, the database can use the log to recover the changes that were made before the crash. The database can read the log and apply the changes to the database, effectively undoing the effects of the crash. This ensures that the database is in a consistent state, even after a crash.\nThe write-ahead log also helps to prevent data loss in the event of a crash. If a change is made to the database and the database crashes before the change is written to the database, the change is still recorded in the log. When the database is restarted, it can read the log and apply the change, ensuring that the data is not lost.\nIn summary, a write-ahead log is a critical component of a database's crash recovery mechanism. It ensures that the database can recover from a crash by providing a record of all changes made to the database, and it helps to prevent data loss by ensuring"
